  {
    "type": "updateEntity",
    "id": {
      "$bytes": "02ae26fa17004ad68a20aad500e8a700"
    },
    "set": [
      {
        "property": {
          "$bytes": "e3e363d1dd294ccb8e6ff3b76d99bc33"
        },
        "value": {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "## Interpreting the preschool debate\n\nThe policy Claims express competing judgments about expanding publicly funded preschool; they are not recommendations attributed to the paper. Their linked estimates come from one historical experiment and do not establish the returns of every new preschool program.\n\nThe comparison holds the pooled sample, 3% discount rate, 50% tax-financing welfare loss and $13,000 murder valuation fixed. Table 7 uses separate arrest ratios and reports modeled benefit/cost ratios of 5.4 (SE 2.2) through age 40 and 7.1 (SE 2.3) through age 65. The latter reuses the existing Table 1 Claim. These are sensitivity cases, not independent studies or confidence bounds. The age-40 model still includes imputation and economic valuation; later benefits require extrapolation.\n\nSource: [Heckman and colleagues (2010)](https://jenni.uchicago.edu/Spencer_Conference/Papers%202010/Heckman_Moon_etal_2010_Rate%20of%20Return%20Perry.pdf), Section 4.3 and Table 7, pp.125–126.",
          "language": {
            "$bytes": "090adac0fca4822e8e719263e67620ec"
          }
        }
      }
    ],
    "unset": []
  },
